    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 230442\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n942 PM PDT Tue Sep 22 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n* Cooler temperatures and lingering light showers and isolated \n  thunderstorms for portions of the southern Great Basin this \n  afternoon.\n\n* Above normal temperatures return midweek onward, with a return\n  of thunderstorm chances for Mohave County and the Lower \n  Colorado River Valley late week.\n\n* Potential pattern shift early next week could bring cooler\n  temperatures and precipitation chances to the area.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...Through Monday.\n\nThe shortwave responsible for yesterday's unsettled weather and \ntoday's cooler temperatures continues its eastward progression\ninto Utah this afternoon. Near the core of this system, \nincreased ascent and lingering wrap-around moisture is expected to \nyield isolated showers and perhaps a thunderstorm or two this \nafternoon, primarily across the southern Great Basin, including \nportions of central Nye County and northern Lincoln County. \nShowers and storms that develop will be capable of producing light\nto moderate rainfall, brief gusty winds, and lightning strikes, \nthough flooding and strong winds are not expected. There is a \nlower (10-20%) chance for additional showers this afternoon over \nthe Spring Mountains and Sheep Range in Clark County where \ntopography will aid in ascent in the wake of the departing system.\nOtherwise, expect temperatures to top out around 2 to 5 degrees \nbelow normal for most locations across the region this afternoon, \na fitting start to the astronomical fall season!\n\nMidweek onward, increasing thicknesses in the wake of the\ndeparting shortwave will allow for a gradual warming trend, with\nhigh temperatures warming to around 5 degrees above normal by \nFriday and Saturday into the upper 90s and lower 100s across \ndesert valleys (the normal high in Las Vegas this time of year is \n91 degrees). Fortunately, only Minor (and localized Moderate) \nHeatRisk will result. Additionally, while most of the region will \nstay dry, ensembles and deterministic guidance are in agreement \nthat a secondary shortwave will pinch off of the aforementioned \nwave, lingering off of the southern California/Baja Coast before \nejecting northeastward and becoming absorbed in the mean flow late\nweek into the weekend. This feature will be accompanied by an \nuptick in precipitable water values, mainly south and east of \nI-15, as well as a return of thunderstorm chances to southeastern \nportions of the area as early as Thursday afternoon. Weak forcing \nwill limit convective activity both spatially and temporally, \nhowever, isolated to scattered showers and thunderstorms look to \ndevelop over the higher terrain of Mohave County later this week, \nwith some uncertainty regarding whether convection will linger \nover Mohave County beyond Thursday. \n\nLate weekend into early next week, ensembles and cluster analyses  \nare indicating a potential longwave trough that will deepen along \nthe West Coast before slowly progressing inland. However, details\nremain highly uncertain at this time with discrepancy among\nguidance regarding the amplitude and timing of this feature. If \nthis pattern shift materializes, another round of cooler \ntemperatures can be expected, along with a return of precipitation\nchances for portions of the region. So, stay tuned.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 06Z Forecast Package...Light \nwinds continue to affect the terminal through the TAF period. Expect \nwesterly winds to turn more variable into the mid morning before \nbecoming northeasterly by the late morning. These winds to reduces \nslightly into the mid afternoon and become variable through the \nremainder of the TAF period. Mostly clear skies are expected through \nthe mid week.\n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast \nCalifornia...For the 06Z Forecast Package...Mostly clear skies and \nlight winds are expected across the region. Winds turn slightly more \nmoderate for KIFP in the mid morning and KBIH by the late morning, \nbut only look to peak around 10 kts. Wind directions look to be \nmostly influenced by local effects and not by larger scale weather \npatterns, meaning that there is the potential for a good amount of \nvariability in directions especially with areas seeing very light \nwinds.\n\n\n&&\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...Phillipson\nAVIATION...Murdock\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
